#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2f40eb3-6087-5c38-b7c6-e160ff925bc6" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Linear Correlation Coefficients in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7ce13a-2f5f-55f5-bac0-ced1a321d5b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5711" :question/problem "A stall selling lemonade is set up at a special four-day summer festival. The stall owner wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x$, the temperature at $12\\,\\textrm{p.m}$, and $y$, the number of cups of lemonade sold that day. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Temperature at<br>$12$ p.m. ($^\\circ \\mathrm{C}$)** | **$26$** | **$30$** | **$22$** | **$18$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Cups of Lemonade Sold** | $100$ | $118$ | $124$ | $86$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overline{x}=24$ and $\\overline{y}=107$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\rho\\approx 0.54$\n2. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n3. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y$.\n\n*Hint: $\\mid \\rho \\mid > 0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3 \\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is *strong*\n- if $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is *weak*\n- if $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho$, we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n$S_{xy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)); = (26 - 24)(100 - 107) + (30 - 24)(118 - 107); = + (22 - 24)(124 - 107) + (18 - 24)(86 - 107); = (2)(-7) + (6)(11) + (-2)(17) + (-6)(-21); =-14 + 66 - 34 + 126; = 144$\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n$S_{xx}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}; = (26 - 24)^{2} + (30 - 24)^{2} + (22 - 24)^{2} + (18 - 24)^{2}; = 4 + 36 + 4 + 36; = 80; S_{yy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}; = (100 - 107)^{2} + (118 - 107)^{2} + (124 - 107)^{2} + (86 - 107)^{2}; = 49 + 121 + 289 + 441; = 900$\nFinally, we use the formula:\n$ρ(x, y)|= \\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80} \\cdot \\sqrt{900}}; \\approx \\frac{144}{8.944272 \\cdot 30}; \\approx 0.54$,\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/id #uuid "5002af3e-d0f0-59eb-a52d-bfd7d101115e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5725" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{6, \\: 3, \\: -1, \\: 0 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{5, \\: 7, \\: -2, \\: 6 \\big\\}$\nYou're given that $\\overline{x}=2$, $\\overline{y}=4$, $\\sigma_x \\approx 2.739$, and $\\sigma_y \\approx 3.536$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov(x, y) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(6 - 2)(5 - 4) + (3 - 2)(7 - 4) + (-1 - 2)(-2 - 4) + (0 - 2)(6 - 4)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1) + (1)(3) + (-3)(-6) + (-2)(2)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[4 + 3 + 18 - 4] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 5.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{5.25}{2.739 \\cdot 3.536} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd2caf12-80c2-553a-9737-64151565cf86" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Linear Correlation Coefficient Using the Expanded Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "8c2381d6-a9af-5ce3-aa20-d0e19a7a7229" :question/math-academy-id "e-9710" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n$x = \\big\\{13, \\: 10, \\: 9, \\: 12 \\big\\},\\qquad y = \\big\\{-1, \\: 3, \\: 2, \\: 4 \\big\\}$\nYou are given that $\\overline{x}=11$ and $\\overline{y}=2$. Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)(-1 - 2) + (10 - 11)(3 - 2) \\\\\n&= + (9 - 11)(2 - 2) + (12 - 11)(4 - 2) \\\\\n&= (2)(-3) + (-1)(1) + (-2)(0) + (1)(2) \\\\\n&=-6 - 1 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (13 - 11)^{2} + (10 - 11)^{2} + (9 - 11)^{2} + (12 - 11)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 + 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 10 \\\\\nS_{yy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= (-1 - 2)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (4 - 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 9 + 1 + 0 + 4 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-5))/(\\sqrt{10} \\cdot \\sqrt{14}) \\\\\n&\\approx - \\frac{5}{3.162278 \\cdot 3.741657} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76569338-1e2a-5b12-96ef-685d272343cd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Correlation Coefficient Using Covariance and Standard Deviations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98692c5f-37d3-5a0f-930e-cb9d9b4520b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ =-0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093537} {:answer/id #uuid "afdb47a2-d0ed-5d21-9d9a-e1a39e6ef77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093538} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc8f31-029d-5a00-aa57-f5873a3f9d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ = 0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093539} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4c2c3d-ab04-5d79-9f09-78335d2dd61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ =-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093540} {:answer/id #uuid "3309670c-8f47-5743-834c-bf27120f0f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ρ =-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093541}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d47e0d00-6213-58d4-b4c4-0da7914c3883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093536}] :question/id #uuid "2811c370-5b30-5984-8372-c002bcffbf44" :question/math-academy-id "q-10271" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $Cov(x, y) =-6$ and their standard deviations are $σ_{x} = 2$ and $σ_{y} = 5$. Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
